Emotion are an important aspect in general human understanding as they lead to better understanding of conversation and other daily behavior. Many psychologist defined rules for emotion detection like different movements of muscles in faces or significant changes in the voice while speaking. Therefore many patterns were observed and aligned with a specfic set of emotions.

Deep learning is a researched topic, known for many kinds of pattern recognition. Nowadays transformer models like ChatGPT are getting more and more popularity and they are said to be all-purpose tools for many tasks. This thesis is implementing state-of-the art transformers for emotion detection in text, audio and video. These were not coverd yet by research in emotion detection with artificial intelligence. Furthermore it compares these with traditional convolution models and gets in touch with multimodal recognition, where the results are combined together through their labels. 
// Transformer wurden vorher noch nicht betrachet

The results are showing, that transformer architectures are able to recognize emotions.  Classification in videos showed by far very promising results. However this thesis showed that emotions are context specific and the state-of-the art models nor the convolution models are able to recognize these beyond their dataset.
